6: ## C1. Data and the actual scalar family
7: 
8: We consider the literal whole-fibre construction in E26 Remark 8.5
9: on a fixed rational elliptic surface with Mordell--Weil group
10: \(\mathbb ZG\). All source coefficients, the selected zero, the
11: finite root actions, the line lifts, the collar data and the
12: Mumford fan remain fixed while the scalar multiplying the chosen
13: Hom section varies. F50 supplies the actual inverse-action map
14: for the expanding family, and we first use its contracting
15: coordinate \(\lambda\). Replacing the marked generator by its
16: negative through F50's displayed source map puts the generic
17: line point and translating point in the form \(aG,bG\) with
18: \(a,b>0\).
19: 
20: The complete source list in K20--21 consists of the following
21: sixteen retained profiles and twenty-six pairs. Here \(d\)
22: denotes the denominator of \(h(G)=1/d\). The symbols in each
23: profile are the actual Kodaira fibres of the source.
24: 
25: | OS entry | \(d\) | Source profile | \((a,b)\) |
26: |---|---:|---|---|
27: | 43 | 2 | \(III^*+3I_1\) | \((1,2),(2,1)\) |
28: | 43 | 2 | \(III^*+II+I_1\) | \((1,2),(2,1)\) |
29: | 45 | 8 | \(I_8+4I_1\) | \((8,1)\) |
30: | 45 | 8 | \(I_8+II+2I_1\) | \((8,1)\) |
31: | 47 | 14 | \(I_7+I_2+3I_1\) | \((14,1)\) |
32: | 47 | 14 | \(I_7+I_2+II+I_1\) | \((14,1)\) |
33: | 47 | 14 | \(I_7+III+2I_1\) | \((7,2),(14,1)\) |
34: | 49 | 6 | \(IV^*+I_2+2I_1\) | \((2,3),(6,1)\) |
35: | 49 | 6 | \(IV^*+III+I_1\) | \((1,6),(2,3),(3,2),(6,1)\) |
36: | 50 | 12 | \(I_1^*+I_3+2I_1\) | \((6,2)\) |
37: | 50 | 12 | \(I_1^*+IV+I_1\) | \((2,6),(6,2)\) |
38: | 55 | 20 | \(I_5+I_4+3I_1\) | \((20,1)\) |
39: | 55 | 20 | \(I_5+I_4+II+I_1\) | \((20,1)\) |
40: | 56 | 30 | \(I_5+I_3+I_2+2I_1\) | \((30,1)\) |
41: | 56 | 30 | \(I_5+I_3+III+I_1\) | \((15,2),(30,1)\) |
42: | 56 | 30 | \(I_5+IV+I_2+I_1\) | \((10,3),(30,1)\) |
43: 
44: For each row choose one of its actual \(I_1\) fibres as the simple
45: whole zero. The generic Picard line is represented by
46: \(\mathcal O(aG-O)\). At the \(I_1^*\) source use the actual
47: corrected divisor \(\mathcal O(aG-O-L_0)\) of F36/F38, with its
48: specified component \(L_0\). All additional allowed vertical
49: source divisors are carried by the actual maps of F52.
50: 
51: Put every additive fibre in its specified root disc. Its
52: effective order and reduced minimal root type are given below.
53: The variable \(j\) in each entry is independent.
54: 
55: | Source fibre and allowed translating coefficient | Effective order | Minimal root fibre |
56: |---|---|---|
57: | \(III^*,\ b=1\) | \(4j+3,\ j\geq0\) | \(III\) |
58: | \(III^*,\ b=2\) | \(4j,\ j\geq1\) | smooth |
59: | \(IV^*,\ b=1,2\) | \(3j+2,\ j\geq0\) | \(IV\) |
60: | \(IV^*,\ b=3,6\) | \(3j,\ j\geq1\) | smooth |
61: | \(I_1^*,\ b=2,6\) | \(2\) | \(I_2\) |
62: | \(II\) | \(6j+1,\ j\geq0\), or \(6j,\ j\geq1\) | \(II\), or smooth |
63: | \(III,\ b=1,3\) | \(4j+1,\ j\geq0\) | \(III\) |
64: | \(III,\ b=2,6\) | \(4j,\ j\geq1\) | smooth |
65: | \(IV,\ b=1,2\) | \(3j+1,\ j\geq0\) | \(IV\) |
66: | \(IV,\ b=3,6\) | \(3j,\ j\geq1\) | smooth |
67: 
68: There are at most two additive discs in these profiles. Label
69: their orders \(m_\infty,m_0\), placing a starred fibre at infinity.
70: For a single additive disc take \(m_0=1\) at an actual regular
71: disc; with no additive disc use two actual regular discs of
72: order one. These regular discs are the marked base charts in
73: K32's construction. The order-one reduced \(II,III,IV\) entries
74: are included in the table.
75: 
76: Keep only coprime \(m_\infty,m_0\). This removes the
77: \(III^*+II\) good/good branch, whose orders are both even.
78: At \(I_1^*+IV\), it selects the reduced orders \(6j+1\),
79: and the good orders \(3j\) with \(j\) odd. In every other
80: two-disc row it imposes the displayed integer gcd condition
81: on the two independently chosen orders.
82: 
83: Every other smooth or multiplicative logarithmic disc has
84: effective order one. Its integer norm \(n_j\), its actual
85: elliptic translation and its finite line character are
86: retained. Write \(b_0\) for the marked ordinary base framing,
87: and put
88: \[
89:  N=\sum_j n_j-b_0,\qquad
90:  M=m_\infty m_0,\qquad
91:  D=m_0n_\infty+m_\infty n_0+MN.                 \tag{SC.1}
92: \]
93: The complete sphere condition is \(D=+1\) or \(D=-1\).
94: All its integral solutions are allowed with their actual
95: cap and collar data. In particular it forces
96: \(\gcd(m_i,n_i)=1\). The parameter used in this catalogue is
97: the actual closed canonical cover
98: \[
99:                          \lambda=r^M.          \tag{SC.2}
100: \]
101: 
102: There is an exact description of every norm solution. Choose
103: integers \(u,v\) with \(m_0u+m_\infty v=1\). For every
104: \(N,j\in\mathbb Z\) and \(\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}\), put
105: \[
106:  n_\infty=\varepsilon u+m_\infty j,\qquad
107:  n_0=\varepsilon v-m_0(j+N).
108: \]
109: Substitution gives \(D=\varepsilon\). Conversely the same
110: equation modulo \(m_\infty\) gives
111: \(n_\infty\equiv\varepsilon u\pmod {m_\infty}\), so the
112: displayed \(j\) is integral and determines the displayed
113: \(n_0\). With \(m_0=1\), retain the arbitrary regular-disc
114: norm \(n_0\) and use
115: \(n_\infty=\varepsilon-m_\infty(n_0+N)\).
116: With both orders one the equation is
117: \(n_\infty+n_0+N=\varepsilon\).
118: These are actual integer norm formulas with every
119: order-one slot present.
120: 
121: ## C2. The page, line and return for each entry
122: 
123: Let \(e_\infty,e_0\) be the source Euler numbers at the two
124: marked discs, and \(e'_\infty,e'_0\) those of their reduced
125: minimal root models. A regular disc has both Euler numbers
126: zero. The table uses
127: \[
128:  e(II)=2,\quad e(III)=3,\quad e(IV)=4,\quad
129:  e(I_1^*)=7,\quad e(IV^*)=8,\quad e(III^*)=9,
130:  \quad e(I_2)=2.
131: \]
132: K32's exact base map absorbs the order-one norms into
133: \(n'_\infty=n_\infty+m_\infty N\). Its actual page is the
134: compactification of
135: \[
136:  \eta^{m_0}-\tau\xi^{m_\infty}=1,\qquad
137:  t=\tau+\xi^{-m_\infty},\qquad
138:  g(C)=\frac{(m_\infty-1)(m_0-1)}2.               \tag{SC.3}
139: \]
140: Its degree over the source base is \(M\). Pull back the
141: source section and the actual root line through the
142: sheet-zero maps, retaining the divisor corrections
143: at every root resolution. The resulting compact page
144: surface and its relative line satisfy
145: \[
146:  \begin{aligned}
147:  \chi&=\frac{M(12-e_\infty-e_0)
148:                  +m_0e'_\infty+m_\infty e'_0}{12},
149:  &H&=\frac{Ma^2}{d},\\
150:  c_1(N_{\rm page})_{\rm rel}^2&=-H,
151:  &p_{\rm rel}&=-24\chi.
152:  \end{aligned}                                  \tag{SC.4}
153: \]
154: The reduced root line has zero degree on each component.
155: Its marked section has height \(H\); the full intersection
156: formula, including every reducible-fibre correction,
157: is the proved formula of F2. Thus (SC.4) determines the
158: actual page and line even when a section intersection
159: alone does not specify its relative square.
160: 
161: For one positive \(r\)-circle, the actual reference Cox
162: product has
163: \[
164:  \begin{split}
165:  (k_{\rm Jin},\ell_{\rm Jin})&=(-3H,-24\chi),\\
166:  (m_{\rm rel},n_{\rm rel},A)&=
167:            (6\chi,3H-6\chi,\chi-H/2).
168:  \end{split}                                   \tag{SC.5}
169: \]
170: These formulas hold for both values of \(D\); the positive
171: Hopf angle is the signed physical suspension angle in
172: K28/K32, and their sheet and Thom maps give the same
173: complex orientation in (SC.5).
174: 
175: For an allowed index-one unimodular periodic fan
